      "text": "To solve the problem, let's define the variables:\n- Let \\( p \\) be the cost of one pen in cents.\n- Let \\( q \\) be the cost of one pencil in cents.\n\nWe are given two equations based on the problem statement:\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost \\$2.24, which is 224 cents. So, the equation for pens and pencils is:\n   \\[\n   4p + 3q = 224\n   \\]\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost \\$1.54, which is 154 cents. So, the equation for pens and pencils is:\n   \\[\n   2p + 5q = 154\n   \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4p + 3q = 224 \\\\\n2p + 5q = 154\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate \\( p \\) by making the coefficients of \\( p \\) the same in both equations. We can do this by multiplying the second equation by 2:\n\\[\n2(2p + 5q) = 2 \\cdot 154\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10q = 308\n\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n4p + 3q = 224 \\\\\n4p + 10q = 308\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( p \\):\n\\[\n(4p + 10q) - (4p + 3q) = 308 - 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4p + 10q - 4p - 3q = 84\n\\]\n\\[\n7q = 84\n\\]\n\\[\nq = \\frac{84}{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nq = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of one pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
